y's dominant fundraising advantage, and a Likely R consensus among most raters point to a clear but not overwhelming Republican edge. Democratic-sponsored polling showing only a 4-point Carey lead and critical headlines on Medicaid and SNAP cuts suggest enough vulnerability to keep this short of Safe R.", "watch": "A late Democratic outside-spending surge or another poll showing Leonard within the margin could shift this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 47 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Carey (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Republican Mike Carey seeks reelection in an Ohio district that leans Republican by about five points. He holds a commanding financial advantage, with roughly $2.1 million on hand compared to about $252,000 for Democrat Don Leonard, who has raised a respectable but far smaller sum. Most expert raters place the race at Likely R, though a few call it Lean R, and prediction markets give Carey roughly a three-in-four chance of winning.\n\nDemocratic-sponsored polling from Hart Research shows Carey leading by four to five points, with a hypothetical matchup closer than the real one. Headlines highlight criticism of Carey over cuts to SNAP, Medicaid, and education, and national Democrats have advertised in the district, suggesting genuine but so far insufficient Democratic momentum. The forecast expects Carey to win by a moderate margin, with meaningful uncertainty given thin and dated polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1732, "margin": -5.63}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.185, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1541, "margin": -7.0}]}